## Package comparison

The package remains aligned with approved Phase 3. This round made no product or test changes, so the unresolved findings from the prior review remain unchanged despite the green verification rerun.

## Findings

### High — Post-bind faults remain unobserved through the Promise facade

`startMusicSessionServer` still never evaluates `service.awaitFailure`. Under this supported facade, a post-bind server `error` only enters `serverFaults`; it neither closes the scope nor reaches a returned Promise. The coder result explicitly identifies this as residual work. Integrate fault supervision into the facade's single scoped Effect graph without adding an isolated runtime, and prove it through deterministic error injection.

### High — The required lifecycle and error matrix remains substantially absent

The suite remains seven facade tests and still lacks the narrow Node-operation/lifecycle-hook constructor and exact counters required by the package. Missing evidence includes direct Layer/provider/source/listener acquisition; complete two-client hello/status/state replay; blocked command and blocked sample shutdown; acceptance racing close; no late writes; connection-defect observation; injected close/unlink/`ENOENT`; cleanup continuation and memoized failed-close outcomes; and signal-handler removal. These are explicit Phase 3 acceptance gates and cannot be deferred.

### Medium — Lifecycle instrumentation remains global, one-shot, and public

`makeConnectionLifecycle` creates one pair of Promises for the whole server. `finalized` resolves on the first connection finalizer and `forwardersFinalized` resolves after any two forwarder completions globally, so the seam cannot identify a specific connection or support exact counts across the required races. The test-only methods are also added to exported `MusicSessionServer` rather than supplied through the package's requested narrow in-file test-hook constructor.

### Medium — Connection-defect diagnostics remain lossy and untested

Non-interruption causes are contained after being offered to a capacity-one sliding queue. Concurrent failures can overwrite an earlier cause before observation, and no synchronous counter proves every contained defect was recorded. Existing tests cover ordinary client disposal rather than an injected socket/write defect.

## Verification

The coder reports 7 server tests, 65 coordinator/provider tests, package gates, and static scans passing. Green baseline checks do not substitute for the missing deterministic Phase 3 evidence above.
